1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
Hi all, I'm Massimiliano Schettino and today we will talk about CAPL.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:11,000
CAPL is an acronym and it stands for CAN ACCESS programming language.

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:20,000
The CAPL is a C-like programming language and it is a very powerful instrument we have when we want to execute test cases

4
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:29,000
or when we want to simulate or emulate electronic control units while we are using vector tools like CANALYZER or CANO.

5
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:38,000
The first thing you should know about CAPL is that it is a procedural language. It is controlled by events.

6
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:48,000
We will have several program blocks or so-called event procedures that will be executed when a certain event will occur.

7
00:00:48,000 --> 00:01:03,000
The type of events we are talking about are key press event, occurrence of messages, CAN, LIN or Ethernet or some action that occurs cyclically such as the timers.

8
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:22,000
Here you can see a CANO configuration window and in order to write a CAPL code we have to access the CAPL browser and we can do that directly from the session tool or by double-clicking a program nodding configuration.

9
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:27,000
This is what you can see when you open a vector CAPL browser.

10
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:42,000
On the left side you can find the F3 with all the defined variables while on the right side you can find a list of events and system and you can drag and drop the events from the list inside the code.

11
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:51,000
In simple tab you can find also network messages and signal and again you can drag and drop them directly into the code.

12
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:57,000
As you can see in central window you can write your code.

13
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:04,000
If you open a blank script it contains just an int block and a variable one.

14
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:13,000
The first one will allow you to add libraries to the code while the second one is useful to define and initialize your variables.

15
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:17,000
And after the variables block you can continue writing your code.

16
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:36,000
One of the first important features of the CAPL is that once you have configured canalizer or CANO and you have linked a CAN database the CAPL browser will recognize the messages and signals names directly from the database.

17
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:43,000
And so you can choose the message you are interested in directly while you are typing its name.

18
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:50,000
Each CAPL code consists of three distinct parts.

19
00:02:50,000 --> 00:03:00,000
The first one the global variable declaration is the part where you declare and initialize the variable you will see when you use in the rest of the code.

20
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:09,000
The second one event procedures is the main part of the code where you write the actions to be performed when an event occurs.

21
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:16,000
The third one is the user defined function where you can define and create your own custom functions.

22
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:20,000
Let's talk about the first one the variables.

23
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:25,000
In CAPL we can find the same data types of C language.

24
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:31,000
It has the integer variables floating point numbers or the declaration of arrays.

25
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:38,000
But we can find also different variables such as CAN message and TIMERS.

26
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:46,000
All the variables except for the timer can or cannot be initialized in the declaration.

27
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:55,000
If a variable is not manually initialized, it will be automatically initialized by the compiler, usually to the zero value.

28
00:03:55,000 --> 00:04:08,000
Today we will not focus on integer variables and long point numbers, but we will focus our attention on the other one, CAN messages and TIMERS.

29
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:20,000
There are two types of TIMERS, the ones based in seconds that are called simply TIMERS and ones based in milliseconds called MS TIMERS.

30
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:23,000
They have to be initialized.

31
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:29,000
We have no other choice, otherwise we cannot use a timer inside the code.

32
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:34,000
Obviously their scope is to generate time events.

33
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:50,000
We will define them in variables, as you can see in the example, by writing TIMER or MS TIMER and writing the name T or T1.

34
00:04:50,000 --> 00:05:01,000
Then, inside our code, we will initialize the timer giving them a value with the proper function called SETTIMER.

35
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:15,000
In the example, we initialize the second timer T to 10 seconds and the milliseconds timer T1 to 100 milliseconds.

36
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:20,000
When we initialize a timer, the timer starts.

37
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:38,000
For what concerns the other variables, the messages, we can declare them thanks to their message identifier, if we have no database, or also to their message name if the database has been linked to the configuration.

38
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:52,000
As you can see in the example, we can use the identifier in hexadecimal or in decimal, we can use the name inside the database, or we can decide to define it later.

39
00:05:52,000 --> 00:06:01,000
But in each case, we define also a name, M1, 2, 3, or 4, that we will use inside the code.

40
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:10,000
After we have defined a message, we can have access to different information of it.

41
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:30,000
As you can see here, if you write the name you gave to the message, in this case M1, and dot, you can have access to the message identifier, to the data linked code.

42
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:39,000
To the direction of the transmission, to the remote transmission request, or to the time points and units.

43
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:49,000
Now that we have finished with the overview on the variables, we can continue with the main part of the CAPL code.

44
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:55,000
